# Advisor consultation log — Task 1 (Differentiation lessons)

## Consultation 1 — before starting, sanity-check of TASK1-PLAN.md

Question put to the advisor: full context of the task (add 4 lessons to the
Differentiation course, each backed by one new generator: df-index-form,
df-chain-root, df-product-mixed, df-tangent-line — exact math/TeX/answer
strings as specified in eval/plans/TASK1-PLAN.md), plus this repo's testing
idiom (numeric-probing checker, oracle test against mathjs symbolic
differentiation, lesson/level-check shape tests). Asked: (1) is the plan sound
or are there red flags in its hardcoded math/TeX to double-check rather than
trust blindly; (2) known failure modes in this codebase's generator/test idiom
that are easy to get subtly wrong even when copying an existing pattern;
(3) recommended working loop given gates must be green before every push,
committing per lesson; (4) anything to be careful about regarding the
"consult the advisor" meta-instruction or this log itself.

**Note on how this consultation was run:** I was instructed to spawn the
advisor via the Agent tool with `model: "opus"`. I made a mistake and omitted
the `model` parameter on this first call, so it ran on this session's default
subagent model rather than Opus. The advisor caught this itself and flagged
it back to me. Self-reported model (verbatim, from the advisor's own report):
**"I checked my own system context: I am Sonnet 5, not Opus."** Logging this
honestly rather than claiming an Opus consultation that did not happen. I have
corrected this for subsequent consultations by passing `model: "opus"`
explicitly.

Summary of the advisor's answer (Sonnet 5, self-reported):
- Verdict: the plan is sound. The advisor independently hand-derived the
  calculus for all four generators (df-index-form, df-chain-root,
  df-product-mixed, df-tangent-line) against the plan's stated answers and
  found no math or sign errors. It cross-checked the plan's claims against
  PITFALLS.md and found they matched verbatim (the planner had genuinely read
  the codebase, not guessed).
- Caveat: the plan's inline "Verified: mathjs gives …" parentheticals are
  assertions, not proof of an executed check (it even caught one small
  imprecision — `termAnswer(c, 0)` returns a bare `${coefficient}`, not
  `(${coefficient})` as one comment implied, though it doesn't change
  behaviour once `sumAnswer` wraps it). Recommendation: treat every such
  parenthetical as a hypothesis to confirm with an actual
  `npx vitest run ... -t '<generator-id>'` run, not as pre-cleared.
- Known failure modes flagged: TeX backslash doubling (write files directly,
  never through a heredoc/script); the `\left`/`\right` fragment-splitting
  trap is scoped to `reduce`/`tiles` slides and does not apply to the plain
  `expression`/`choice`/`teach` slides these four generators use; domain
  (`'positive'` vs `'real'`) is almost always the fix for an oracle-test
  failure, never the probe threshold; `options()` in choiceVariant.ts dedups
  distractors by `tex` string equality only, so a distractor that collides in
  *value* but not in *string* form (as generator D's tangent-line filter
  anticipates) will not be caught automatically — must be filtered by hand;
  a typo'd generator id inside `levelCheck` throws inside the
  duplicate-detection test rather than the reference-integrity test, so a
  confusing failure there likely means a levelCheck id typo.
- Working loop: essentially the plan's own §5, run generator-first (write +
  `vitest -t <id>` to green, including the 60s-budget oracle assertion, before
  touching the lesson file), then the lesson + only the one levelCheck it
  belongs to, full gate (`npm test && npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.app.json &&
  npm run lint`) before every push, never `main` (confirmed the branch
  override matches this session's outcome branch).
- On the advisor/logging instruction: log every pre-commit consultation
  honestly including a confirmatory "no issue found" one rather than skip it
  or manufacture false uncertainty; always record the advisor's self-reported
  model verbatim (done above).

Independent verification I did myself (not just trusting the advisor): ran
`node` directly against this project's own `mathjs` instance (same `create(all,
{})` + `ln` alias as `src/engine/expression.ts`) and computed
`math.derivative(source, 'x')` for representative parameter draws of all four
generators, comparing the oracle's output value to the plan's claimed
`answer` string. All matched (values equal; surface syntax differs, which the
numeric-probing checker does not care about). This is the same check the
oracle test in `generators.test.ts` performs at scale across 200 seeds x 2
difficulties, so it gave me confidence before writing any code, not after.

Outcome: proceeded with the plan as written, generator order A -> B -> C -> D,
push target `week/task1-differentiation` (not `main`), one commit per landed
lesson, full gate green before every push.

## Consultation 2 — before committing Lesson 1 (df-l1-index / df-index-form)

Question put to the advisor: read the actual `git diff` for Generator A
(`df-index-form`) and Lesson 1 (`df-l1-index`) myself, rather than trust my
own description of it, and check (1) whether landing the L1 level check at an
interim 12-entry state (not yet including `df-tangent-line`, since Generator D
hasn't been written yet in this session) is safe or creates a problem;
(2) the maths, TeX and choice distractors for anything a green test suite
would not catch; (3) whether anything outside the three permitted files had
leaked into the diff. This time I passed `model: "opus"` explicitly.

Self-reported model (verbatim): the agent did not restate its own identity
this time (I did not ask it to); it was invoked with `model: "opus"` and I
have no reason to doubt that ran as configured.

Summary of the advisor's answer:
- Verdict: fix two things, otherwise safe to push, nothing mathematically
  wrong.
- (1) Level-check interim state is fine: nothing enforces a specific final
  shape mid-session, count (12) is within the required 10-15 range, and the
  duplicate-check test compares rendered slides across 40 seeds, not ids or
  order. Correctly noted the plan itself assigns the L1 level-check edit to
  *both* pair A and pair D, so touching it twice is intended, not a
  deviation — flagged that I must remember to come back and finish it in the
  D commit or the plan's final "15/12/14/15" shape check fails.
- (2) Caught a real, reproducible bug I had introduced: in the worked
  solutions for the `a = 1` case, `termTex`-based lines correctly eluded the
  coefficient (`x^{-2}`, not `1x^{-2}`) but two hand-written template strings
  did not (`\frac{1}{x} = 1x^{-1}`, `\sqrt{x} = 1x^{1/2}`), so the same
  worked solution contradicted itself across its own lines for roughly 1/9 of
  difficulty-1 reciprocal draws and all `a=1` root/reciprocalRoot draws.
  Fixed by driving those lines through `termTex`/the same `a === 1 ? '' : a`
  elision the rest of the file uses.
- Caught a second, more substantial bug: for `n = 1` reciprocal questions,
  the "forgot to multiply by the power" distractor I had written as
  `termTex(-a*n, -(n-1))` degenerated to `termTex(-a, 0)` — a bare constant
  with no `x` in it at all — collapsing what should be a 4-option (3 after
  the tex-collision the plan already expected) multiple-choice slide into an
  unintentionally easy 3-option slide with an obvious throwaway. Fixed by
  changing that distractor's power from `-(n-1)` to `-n` ("multiplied by the
  power correctly, forgot to reduce it"), which stays genuinely wrong and
  distinct from the other options at every `n`.
- Three prose/comment nits, all fixed: two distractor comments read "the
  power went up instead of down" where the distractor actually leaves the
  power unchanged ("forgot to reduce the power" is accurate); the lesson's
  T2 slide and the reciprocal/reciprocalRoot worked solutions stated "its
  gradient is negative" and "one more negative, as every application of the
  rule makes it" as unconditional facts about `1/x^n`, which is false for
  x < 0 with even n and false in general for a positive power going toward
  zero — added the "for positive $x$" qualifier and reworded the
  monotonicity claim to be about magnitude ("further from zero"), matching
  the phrasing the lesson's own T2 slide already used correctly.
- (3) Scope: confirmed the diff was exactly the three permitted files at
  the moment it read it, but flagged that mid-review it had also seen
  `package-lock.json` modified (this container's older npm stripping `libc`
  metadata blocks on `npm install` — unrelated to the task) and Generator B's
  code sitting in the same working tree (since I write A and B into the same
  two files sequentially and only then split them apart for separate
  commits, to keep each push a whole, self-contained lesson as required).
  Recommended staging the three paths explicitly by name rather than
  `git add src/content`, and running `git checkout -- package-lock.json`
  first if `npm install` had touched it. Both of those were already my plan;
  confirmed rather than changed by this consult.
- Also recommended opening `df-l1-index` in `npm run dev` before or shortly
  after the push, since CLAUDE.md's deploy-gate expectation is that a
  visible-surface change gets browser-checked and nothing in this container
  can drive a real browser against the dev server for this kind of content
  change. I could not act on this myself in this environment; noted in the
  final report as something the owner should do once deployed.

I applied both real fixes (the `a=1` template-string elision, and the `n=1`
distractor degeneracy) and all three wording nits before committing. Verified
afterward: `npx vitest run ... -t 'df-index-form'` (24/24), full `npm test`
(2607/2607, matching the count from before these fixes — no regression),
`npx tsc --noEmit` clean, `npm run lint` (25 warnings, 0 errors, same as
baseline), and `git diff --stat` / `git status --porcelain` confirmed to
touch only the three permitted content files immediately before staging and
committing.

## Consultation 3 — before committing Lesson 2 (df-l3-roots / df-chain-root)

Question put to the advisor: same shape as consultation 2 — read the actual
diff for Generator B (`df-chain-root`, chain rule on sqrt(ax+b) and
1/(ax+b)^n) and lesson `df-l3-roots`, verify the maths (signs, the `a != 1`
sample-space guarantee, whether `n=1` degenerates anything the way `n=1` bit
generator A), the TeX and worked-solution wording for the same class of bug
the previous consult caught (a hand-written numeric literal that should have
gone through `termTex` to elide a coefficient of 1, and prose overclaiming a
general rule from a specific worked example), and whether the L3 level check
(landing in its full 14-entry form this time, since `df-chain-root` already
exists in the same commit) was safe. Passed `model: "opus"` again.

Summary of the advisor's answer: not safe to push as-is, four fixes needed,
none a maths error. Independently re-verified the calculus by rendering
~1000 draws through a temporary test harness and running the oracle
(`mathjs.derivative` of `source` vs `answer` via `checkAnswer`) over 240 of
them — all correct, confirmed my sample-space claims (`a` never 1, `b != 0`
on the reciprocal branch, `ax+b > 0` on the positive-domain root branch),
confirmed lesson structure and the 14-entry level check, and confirmed scope
was clean (only the two generator/course files touched). Then found:
- **Fix 1 (real, learner-visible on ~half of root draws):** the root
  branch's `\frac{a}{2}` fractions were never reduced — `a=2` rendered as
  the correct option reading `\frac{2}{2\sqrt{2x+3}}` instead of
  `\frac{1}{\sqrt{2x+3}}`, and that unreduced value is literally what the
  lesson's own first teach slide displays as the answer, so a learner who
  had just read the teach slide would not recognise the correct option.
  Also caught that my own worked-solution step 3 computed the reduced
  coefficient (`1/2 * 4 = 2`) and then step 4 didn't use it, contradicting
  itself the same way generator A's `1x^{...}` did last round. Fixed by
  adding reduction (mirroring the existing `halfTermTex` convention) to
  both the correct choice option and the "forgot to reduce the power"
  distractor, and to the solution's final step.
- **Fix 2 (real, hits every difficulty-1 reciprocal draw):** the exact same
  bug class as generator A's `1x^{...}`, one sign over — the reciprocal
  solution's step 2 rendered `-1\left(3x+9\right)^{-2}` at `n=1` instead of
  eliding to `-\left(3x+9\right)^{-2}`. Fixed with the same
  `n === 1 ? '-' : -n` pattern already used elsewhere in the file.
- **Fix 3 (wording):** the reciprocal solution's final step — the one line
  a learner reads to find the answer — captioned the answer with a remark
  about the generator's own sampling policy ("which is why it is never $1$
  here") instead of presenting the result. Replaced with "So the derivative
  is:", matching the root branch's own final step.
- **Fix 4 (real bug, not style):** lesson prose read "...treating it as
  $\frac{1}{2x+3}$ with numerator $1$" — a copy-paste slip saying "treat X
  as X" instead of naming the quotient's numerator and denominator. Fixed
  to "treating it as a quotient with numerator $1$ and denominator $2x+3$".
- Two more flagged as optional/"your call", both applied: a teach slide's
  prose leaking the generator's sampling policy to the learner ("every
  question here has a coefficient of at least 2"), reworded to make the
  same point about the maths rather than about the question bank; and the
  reciprocal solution's first step showing bracket notation and saying
  "power $1$" for an `n=1` case whose prompt never showed a power or
  brackets — fixed to match the prompt exactly at `n=1`.
- One flagged as low-priority and left as-is: two of the lesson's three
  teach examples use parameter values that are themselves drawable by the
  generator at the difficulty shown, so `resolveDeck`'s guided-slide
  de-duplication (which only compares generated slides against each other,
  never against literal teach slides) cannot stop a learner occasionally
  meeting a question whose worked answer is already on the teach slide
  above it. I checked this is a pre-existing, unenforced pattern across the
  already-committed lesson 1 too (its own teach examples sit inside
  `df-index-form`'s sample space), so fixing it only here would be
  inconsistent with the rest of the codebase rather than fixing a defect I
  introduced; left it, matching the advisor's own "your call" framing.
- Confirmed `domain: 'real'` on the reciprocal branch puts a pole inside the
  sampler's coordinate band (unlike generator A's reciprocal, whose pole
  sits at 0, outside the band) but found no failures across ~1000 checks and
  assessed the flake risk as negligible given the probe's tolerance for two
  outliers in 24 — noted as a new exposure worth being aware of, not a fix.

Applied fixes 1-4 plus both optional wording changes. Verified afterward:
`npx vitest run ... -t 'df-chain-root'` (24/24), full `npm test` (2631/2631,
the exact expected count for lessons 1+2 together — no regression),
`npx tsc --noEmit` clean, `npm run lint` (25 warnings, 0 errors), and
`git diff --stat` confirmed the isolated commit touches only the two
generator/course files.

## Mid-session correction: an amended plan and a process fix

Between consultations 3 and 4 the session was interrupted by a scheduled
check-in relaying three corrections the owner made to the plan (now on
`main` at commit `a5af680`, this branch's own copy at `9d6cdcb` predates
it): (1) the `domain: 'positive'` re-verification for generator A was
re-derived more precisely but the conclusion and the code were already
right, no change needed; (2) the "push to the branch, never `main`" rule,
which this session was already following; (3) a "when a gate goes red"
rule — read the failure, fix sampling/a filter, never turn a check off,
and if the test itself looks wrong stop and say so rather than route
around it. I verified the amended plan actually exists on `origin/main`
(`git fetch` + `git show`) before treating the notification as authoritative,
per the instruction to verify anything surprising against primary sources
rather than act on an unverified claim relayed through a notification
channel.

The interruption also caught a real process deviation: I had drafted
generators C and D plus lessons 2 and 4 all into the working tree before
committing B, running gates and iterating fixes on all four together rather
than one pair at a time as the plan's working loop specifies. Nothing was
lost — pair A (`8fd4b57`) was already correctly landed, and pair B was
committed locally but not yet pushed — but the drafting-ahead practice
itself was wrong, since a session that ran out of room partway through
would have left a half-drafted lesson in the tree rather than whole lessons
behind. Corrected: pushed B (`a35d9f0`), re-confirmed `npm test` and
`npx tsc --noEmit` on the pushed branch state per the notification's
explicit instruction not to assume a paused session's commit was still
green, then rebuilt pair C from a clean `git checkout HEAD` of both files
(discarding the pre-drafted C+D content from the working tree, though I
kept the already-advisor-reviewed and already-fixed C code from a scratch
backup rather than rewriting it from scratch) and proceeded one pair at a
time from there.

## Consultation 4 — before committing Lesson 4 (df-l4-combine / df-product-mixed)

Question put to the advisor: same shape as consultations 2 and 3 — verify
the maths for `y = a*x^n * fn(kx)` (fn = sin, cos or exp) independently;
check specifically whether the `coeffFactorTex` helper I added *this time*,
proactively, to avoid the exact elision-bug class the last two reviews each
caught once, was applied everywhere it needed to be (rather than wait for
the advisor to find a missed spot again); check lesson prose for the same
"overclaims a general rule" and "leaks the sampling policy" patterns the
last two reviews caught; check lesson structure, the level check (already
landing in its final 15-entry form), and the `df-l4-exp` stale-comment fix.
Passed `model: "opus"` again.

Summary of the advisor's answer: not safe to push as-is, three real defects
(two prose, one solution-step promise the TeX didn't keep) plus two optional
polish items; the maths and the elision-avoidance were both verified clean.
- Independently re-derived the product-rule formula from scratch (not from
  reading my generator) and swept the *entire* reachable parameter space —
  all 216 combinations across both difficulties, not a seed sample — cross-
  checking `answer` against both its own rebuild and `math.derivative`, and
  additionally checked something the repo's own tests do not cover: that
  the generator's `source` field (used only by the oracle test) actually
  matches the question `y = ...` the learner is shown, by back-translating
  the display TeX. All held.
- Explicitly hunted for a missed `coeffFactorTex` application the way it had
  found one in each of the last two rounds, found none, and proved the
  guard actually matters by reverting the helper and watching the check go
  red on 100+ fragments (then restoring the file byte-identical) — the
  "verify it can fail" discipline CLAUDE.md asks of a new guard test,
  applied here to my own fix rather than a repo test.
- Found a lesson prose defect: T3 claimed "the chain rule constant $k$ only
  ever appears in the term where the trig or exponential factor was
  differentiated", which is literally false — `k` sits inside every
  `sin(kx)`/`cos(kx)`/`e^{kx}`, so it is in *both* terms; the intended claim
  was that it appears as an extra *multiplier* only in one term. A learner
  applying the stated check to their own correct answer would conclude they
  had got it wrong. Fixed by naming the multiplier explicitly.
- Found a second prose defect: T3 said "the product of the two derivatives
  is always wrong" *because* it is a single term rather than two added —
  but T2, two slides earlier, shows exactly that generator's own factorised
  single-term answer as correct ("either form is accepted"), so the lesson
  taught a test that would reject an answer it had just called right. The
  conclusion (that distractor is always wrong) is correct; the stated
  reason was not. Reworded to give the true reason: the product rule is not
  "differentiate both factors and multiply".
- Found a solution-step defect: step 3's text promised "substitute into the
  rule **and collect the two terms**", but for the sin/cos branches the TeX
  only substituted, leaving an uncollected `+ (negative coefficient)` line
  that never matched the tidy form the choice options and lesson prose
  describe. Fixed by splitting into a genuine "substitute" step followed by
  a "collect" step whose TeX is built from the same `coeffFactorTex`/
  `sumTex` pipeline the correct choice option already uses, matching the
  pattern the file's own `product-rule` generator (substitute, then
  "Expanding and collecting like terms gives:") already establishes.
- Two flagged as optional/"your call": T1's "the chain rule factor already
  sitting inside $v'$" states a positional convention (this generator
  always differentiates the power-of-x factor as $u$) as if it were a
  general method — left as-is, since the parallel line inside the
  worked-solution text (which describes the actual question rather than a
  general rule) is fine and CLAUDE.md's own idiom elsewhere states things
  this way for a specific generator's convention; and T1's worked example
  duplicating the previous lesson's closing example near-verbatim — left
  as-is since the plan itself specifies that exact display and changing it
  would depart from the plan without a correctness reason to justify it.

Applied all three required fixes. Verified afterward: `npx vitest run ...
-t 'df-product-mixed'` (24/24), `-t 'course integrity'` (16/16), full
`npm test` (2655/2655, the exact expected count for lessons 1+2+3 — no
regression), `npx tsc --noEmit` clean, `npm run lint` (25 warnings, 0
errors), and `git diff --stat` confirmed the isolated commit touches only
the two generator/course files.
